does not modify it and must not be read as doing so. Where the two could be
read as conflicting, `LICENSE` governs and this permission is void to the
extent of the conflict.


The permission
--------------

  As an additional permission under section 7 of the GNU Affero General
  Public License version 3, the copyright holders grant permission to
  distribute compiled binaries of the client application through
  application distribution platforms (including the Apple App Store and
  Google Play), subject to those platforms' terms, and to link the
  application against the proprietary platform SDKs required to function
  on the target device (including Google Play Services and Apple's CarPlay
  framework). This permission applies to distribution of the compiled
  application only; the Corresponding Source remains available under the
  AGPL, and the network-use obligation of section 13 is unaffected.


Why it exists
-------------

Unmodified AGPL terms and the Apple App Store do not coexist. The store's
terms impose usage restrictions and device limits on users of the binary
that GPL-family licences forbid adding — the reason VLC was pulled from the
App Store. Google Play is less pointed but not clean either, and the app
links Google Play Services for the Fused Location Provider, which is
proprietary and is not a system library in the GPL sense.

Section 7 exists for exactly this: the licensor may grant permissions
*beyond* the licence's terms without forking the licence text. The
alternative considered and rejected was split licensing — AGPL for the
server, Apache-2.0 for the client — which would have put a licence boundary
through `DLR.Core`, shared by both, to be policed forever by a test.


What it does not do
-------------------

- It does not touch the server. Modifications to `DLR.Server` remain
  AGPL-3.0-only in full.
- It does not touch section 13. Anyone who lets users interact with a
  modified version of this software over a network must still offer those
  users the Corresponding Source of that running version. That clause is
  the reason this project chose the AGPL, and nothing here erodes it.
- It does not make the compiled application proprietary. The Corresponding
  Source of any binary distributed under this permission remains available
  under the AGPL.


Who has it
----------

Everyone. The permission is part of the project's outbound terms, and
contributions are accepted on inbound-equals-outbound terms
(see `CONTRIBUTING.md`), so it travels with the code: a downstream fork can
ship to the stores on the same footing as the original maintainers. A
permission the maintainer alone enjoyed would be a proprietary carve-out
wearing a section 7 hat, which is not the intent.


Removing it
-----------

Section 7 permits a recipient to remove additional permissions from any
copy they convey, or from any part of it. Nothing here obliges you to keep
this file if you would rather ship under plain AGPL-3.0.


A caveat, stated plainly
------------------------

This wording is engineering judgement about licence mechanics. It is not
legal advice, and the App Store interaction in particular is an area where
reasonable people have landed differently. If you are relying on this
permission for something that matters to you, have it reviewed by someone
qualified.
